<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toruń</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thorn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toruń is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1233 under the German name Thorn, it became a prosperous Hanseatic town. Its citizens joined the revolt against the Order in 1454, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> of 1466 placed it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>; it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1793 and returned to Poland in 1920. The birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, its medieval old town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torun.jpg" alt="Toruń" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Domun · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stare_Miasto,_Ratusz_Staromiejski_w_Toruniu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Toruń stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Archaeologists date the first settlement near it to 1100 BCE, in the Lusatian culture, and there are few traces from the 5th century BCE to the 8th century CE. In the Roman period two settlements and a cemetery have been found, and a river crossing is visible near the village of Stary Toruń. It was first mentioned in 1226 as Thorun, and from the 15th century the form Thorn came into use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 7th to the 13th century an old Slavonic settlement stood at a ford of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, on the site where the Teutonic Castle was later built. In the 10th century it became part of the emerging Polish state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a> and grew into a fortified stronghold, which was destroyed around the turn of the 12th and 13th centuries, most likely in a Prussian raid. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> expanded the town in 1233. From 1264 until 1411 it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and by the 17th century it was a leading trading point, which shaped its architecture from Brick Gothic to Mannerist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> was born there in 1473. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">John I Albert</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, died there in 1501 and his heart was buried in St. John's Cathedral. Toruń became a royal city of Poland in 1506, and a royal mint began work in 1528. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> in 1793 it was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, was part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> from 1807 to 1815, serving as temporary capital in April and May 1809, and then returned to Prussia.</p><p>Under German rule it was a strong centre of Polish resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a>, and the Toruń Scientific Society was founded in 1875. It became Polish again after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and German forces occupied it from 7 September 1939 to 1 February 1945, arresting, deporting and murdering many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, especially the Polish elites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city was spared bombing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and its Old Town and central marketplace are entirely preserved. The medieval part was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1997. It has the largest number of preserved Gothic houses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, as well as the 14th-century cathedral, which holds the Tuba Dei, the largest medieval church bell in Poland. Nicolaus Copernicus University, founded in 1945, is the largest institution of higher education in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toruń” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Prussian Confederation pledged allegiance to Casimir IV on 6 March 1454, the Thirteen Years' War, the War of the Cities, began; the king appointed Hans von Baysen (Jan Bażyński) war-time governor and took oaths from the citizens of <b>Toruń</b>, Elbląg and Königsberg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>New finds continue: in 2019 two large rondel complexes were discovered east of the Vistula near <b>Toruń</b> in Poland.</blockquote><a href="/culture/linear-pottery-culture">Linear Pottery culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After a Protestant crowd vandalised the Jesuit college in <b>Toruń</b> (Thorn) in Royal Prussia, the Polish supreme court blamed Mayor Johann Gottfried Rösner and nine other Lutheran officials for neglect of duty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Poland, Lithuania and the Teutonic Knights made peace at Thorn (<b>Toruń</b>), ending the war in which the Knights had been routed at Grunwald in 1410.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Polish king Casimir IV Jagiellon and the Teutonic Knights made peace at <b>Toruń</b>, ending the longest of the Polish–Teutonic Wars.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-19">19 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
